\section{Executable Computations}
\label{app:qiskit-handles}

This appendix delivers, as runnable Qiskit circuits, the three computations that
\S\ref{sec:interactions} stated as invitations, together with a fourth that arose from them: the
meson's colour skeleton, the two-node companion of the baryon triangle. Each was executed on a statevector simulator and the
outputs below are the verified results of those runs. All three were subsequently run on IBM Quantum
hardware (156-qubit Heron-class processors, 12--13 June 2026, across four devices); each subsection
closes with its hardware record, and the entry-point beta-decay circuit
(Appendix~\ref{app:qiskit}) was validated and run alongside them. We are precise about epistemic status
throughout: execution validates that the framework's structural claims have been \emph{faithfully
encoded} and that the encoded structures behave as asserted; whether the structures are the right
physics is the question the body of the paper argues. The hardware runs add one thing the simulator
cannot: the encoded structures survive contact with a physical device, at fidelities limited by
known, independently characterised noise channels --- and both canonical hadron skeletons, the
baryon triangle and the meson loop, assembled themselves from closed colour wiring on physical
processors. The fidelities track circuit depth as expected: the shallow beta-decay and CKM circuits
return their quantities to a fraction of a percent, while the postselected multi-cap skeletons sit
near a two-setting fidelity of $0.89$, the coherence witnesses softening with each additional
contraction while postselection keeps the populations clean. All run in seconds on a laptop.

\subsection{The CKM-extended charged current}
\label{app:qiskit-ckm}

The beta-decay circuit of \S\ref{sec:interactions} is extended with one generation qubit $g$
($\lvert0\rangle$ = first generation, $\lvert1\rangle$ = second), and the $W^-$ emission is promoted
to the full charged-current gate: the $T_3$ raise on $B_q$ tensored with the CKM rotation on $g$,
taken to be $R_y(2\delta)$ with $\delta = 2/9$ --- the Koide phase, so that the suggestive
identification of Open Problem~\ref{op:cabibbo} becomes an executable hypothesis.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,basicstyle=\small\ttfamily]
DELTA = 2/9
qc = QuantumCircuit(6)        # q0=B_nu q1=B_e q2=g q3=C_q q4=B_q q5=A_q
qc.ry(2*DELTA, 2)             # CKM rotation on the generation register
qc.x(4)                       # T3 raise: d -> up-type
qc.h(1); qc.cx(1, 0)          # W- decays to the lepton Bell pair
psi = Statevector(qc)
\end{lstlisting}

The executed output has amplitude support on exactly four basis states
($\lvert q_5 q_4 q_3 q_2 q_1 q_0\rangle$, little-endian): $\lvert010000\rangle$ and
$\lvert010011\rangle$ at $+0.689719$, and $\lvert010100\rangle$ and $\lvert010111\rangle$ at
$+0.155845$. The structure is readable by eye: two generation branches, each spread over the two
components of the lepton Bell pair. Multiplying back the $\sqrt2$ of the Bell spread, the circuit's
amplitudes \emph{are} the CKM factors:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\hline
 & circuit & PDG & deviation \\
\hline
$\lvert V_{ud}\rvert = \cos(2/9)$ & $0.97541$ & $0.97435$ & $+0.11\%$ \\
$\lvert V_{us}\rvert = \sin(2/9)$ & $0.22040$ & $0.22430$ & $-1.74\%$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
with the $2\times2$ block exactly unitary by construction. Two readings of the run. First, the
$g = \lvert1\rangle$ branch --- the Cabibbo-suppressed transition into the second generation --- is
present in the output with the correct relative amplitude even though real beta decay cannot reach
charm: the circuit computes the internal-structure factor, and the kinematic exclusion belongs to
the spacetime sector. The division of labour of \S\ref{sec:interactions} is thus visible in a single
statevector. Second, the comparison row is the test the framework must pass or fail: a reader who
believes the Cabibbo angle is not $2/9$ can replace one rotation angle and watch the first column
move.

\emph{Hardware record.} Run on \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh} (12 June 2026, 14:15 UTC, 8192 shots,
SamplerV2), extracting the CKM factors from the generation-qubit marginal:
$\lvert V_{ud}\rvert = 0.97534$ and $\lvert V_{us}\rvert = 0.22069$, each within a small fraction
of one shot-noise standard deviation of the ideal encoding values ($0.97541$, $0.22040$); the
Bell-pair witness $P(q_1{=}q_0) = 0.932$ and the appearance of $17$ outcome strings against the
ideal four locate the device's noise floor. The reading requires care: the processor measures the
\emph{circuit}, not the quark, so the per-mille agreement validates the extraction pipeline on
physical hardware, while the physics comparison --- $\sin(2/9)$ against the measured
$\lvert V_{us}\rvert = 0.22430$, low by $1.74\%$ --- is exactly as it stands in the table and is
untouched by where the circuit runs.

\subsection{The GHZ--W interference}
\label{app:qiskit-interference}

The candidate mechanism for the up-quark discrepancy (Open Problem~\ref{op:up-quark}) is
interference between the tripartite (GHZ) and pairwise (W) contributions at the first generation.
This computation makes the interference itself executable: prepare
$\lvert\psi\rangle = \alpha\lvert\mathrm{GHZ}\rangle + \beta e^{\mathrm{i}\varphi}\lvert\mathrm{W}\rangle$,
trace to one qubit, and read the Bloch radius $r$ of the cut --- the base coordinate from which the
geodesic mass proxy is built (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}).

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,basicstyle=\small\ttfamily]
vec = a*GHZ + b*np.exp(1j*phi)*W
qc = QuantumCircuit(3); qc.initialize(vec, [0, 1, 2])
rho = partial_trace(Statevector(qc), [1, 2])     # keep the cut qubit
# r from the Bloch components of rho
\end{lstlisting}

The executed run, cross-checked against the closed form
$r^2 = \tfrac23\alpha^2\beta^2 + \beta^4/9$ to machine precision:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\hline
$\beta^2$ & $r$ (circuit) & $r$ (incoherent) & enhancement \\
\hline
$0.02$ & $0.1145$ & $0.0067$ & $17.2\times$ \\
$0.05$ & $0.1787$ & $0.0167$ & $10.7\times$ \\
$0.10$ & $0.2472$ & $0.0333$ & $7.4\times$ \\
$0.50$ & $0.4410$ & $0.1667$ & $2.6\times$ \\
$1.00$ & $0.3333$ & $0.3333$ & $1.0\times$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
Two findings, both verified in the run. \emph{First-order response}: the cross term makes
$r \approx \sqrt{2/3}\,\alpha\beta$ at small admixture --- linear in the admixture
\emph{amplitude}, where incoherent mixing would be quadratic --- so a few-per-cent W component
moves the mass-relevant invariant by an order of magnitude more than probability-weighting
suggests. That is the right shape for a thirty-per-cent effect from a modest admixture, which is
what the mechanism requires. \emph{Phase rigidity}: $r$ is independent of the relative phase
$\varphi$ to $10^{-17}$ (the cross term has fixed modulus $\alpha\beta/\sqrt6$; $\varphi$ rotates
its direction only), and identical across all three cuts by symmetry. The mechanism, if it is the
right one, therefore lives in the admixture \emph{magnitude}, not its phase --- a sharp constraint
on the formal treatment that Open Problem~\ref{op:up-quark} requests. What this computation does
not supply is the map from $r$ to mass, which is precisely the associator--geodesic reconciliation
of Open Problem~\ref{op:associator-geodesic}; the playground is built, the mechanism awaits its
theorem.

\emph{Hardware record.} Two runs on 12 June 2026, by single-qubit tomography of the cut
($r$ from $\langle X\rangle, \langle Y\rangle, \langle Z\rangle$, EstimatorV2): unmitigated
(resilience level $0$, $4096$ shots, \texttt{ibm\_fez}, 13:10 UTC) and with default readout
mitigation (resilience level $1$, \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh}, 14:04 UTC). Note the two runs differ in
both mitigation and device, so the comparison is indicative rather than controlled.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\hline
$\beta^2$ & analytic & unmitigated & mitigated \\
\hline
$0.02$ & $0.1145$ & $0.1539$ & $0.1248$ \\
$0.05$ & $0.1787$ & $0.2002$ & $0.1772$ \\
$0.10$ & $0.2472$ & $0.2350$ & $0.2789$ \\
$0.50$ & $0.4410$ & $0.4245$ & $0.4719$ \\
$1.00$ & $0.3333$ & $0.3558$ & $0.3373$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The discriminator point $\beta^2 = 0.02$ separates the coherent law ($r = 0.1145$) from incoherent
mixing ($r = 0.0067$) by a factor of seventeen; the device returns $0.154$ and $0.125$ --- factors
of $23$ and $19$ above the incoherent prediction --- so the first-order response is confirmed on
hardware in both runs. The residuals behave as the error model predicts: $r$ is a quadrature sum
of three noisy expectation values and hence positively biased where the true vector is short, and
switching on readout mitigation roughly halved that floor (the $\beta^2 = 0.02$ excess falling
from $+34\%$ to $+9\%$, and the pure-W point, whose transverse components vanish identically, from
$+7\%$ to $+1.2\%$), at the few-per-cent cost of residual scatter mid-sweep, where the two runs
straddle the analytic curve with opposite signs. The mean absolute residual of the mitigated run
is about six per cent across the sweep, and neither run shows the $\beta^2$-linear shape that
incoherent mixing would impose. The natural refinement is the self-calibrating point
$\beta^2 = 0$, where $r = 0$ exactly and the measured value is the noise floor itself, to be
removed in quadrature from the rest of the sweep.

\subsection{Composition laws: fusion, anti-speciality, and the baryon}
\label{app:qiskit-composition}

The dictionary of \S\ref{sec:entanglement-classes} rests on the Coecke--Kissinger classification:
GHZ is a special Frobenius structure (its nodes fuse under composition) and W is anti-special (they
do not). Both laws, and the two composite objects the strong sector builds from them, are
executable. Composition means joining two legs by the Bell cap
$\langle\Phi^+\rvert = (\langle00\rvert + \langle11\rvert)/\sqrt2$, a postselected projection whose
weight we report with each result. The executed results:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,basicstyle=\small\ttfamily]
GHZ3 o GHZ3:  fidelity with GHZ4 = 1.000000   (weight 0.2500)
W3   o W3  :  fidelity with W4   = 0.000000   (weight 0.2778)
   W o W produces: |0000> + |0101> + |0110> + |1001> + |1010>
GHZ triangle: fidelity with GHZ3 = 1.000000   (weight 0.0312)
W triangle  : fidelity with W3   = 0.750000   (weight 0.0185)
Closed loop scalar <GHZ3|triangle> = 1.000000
\end{lstlisting}

Read upward from the wiring. Two GHZ nodes sharing a leg fuse into one GHZ node, exactly --- the
spider-fusion law, here as a fidelity of one. Two W nodes do not: the contraction has \emph{zero}
overlap with $\mathrm{W}_4$, producing instead the vacuum-plus-double-excitation set printed above
--- anti-speciality as an output state one can inspect. The triangle is the construction the paper
has been pointing at since \S\ref{sec:charges}: three nodes pairwise joined by colour edges. For GHZ
nodes the triangle's three free legs carry \emph{exactly} the state $\lvert\mathrm{GHZ}_3\rangle$,
fidelity $1.000000$: the statement that a quark's $C$-qubit is GHZ-entangled with its partners in a
hadron is not an assumption about baryons but an output of the composition law --- the baryon is
the wiring. The W triangle does not close onto $\mathrm{W}_3$ (fidelity $0.75$), the executable
face of the law that, in the dictionary, separates the binding sector from the non-binding one.
Capping the baryon's free legs closes the loop entirely, and the resulting scalar --- the singlet
weight of the closed colour cycle, the skeleton on which the glueball discussion of
\S\ref{sec:predictions} is built --- is exactly $1$. Organising the spectrum of such closed loops
is the ZX-calculus computation stated as the third invitation in \S\ref{sec:interactions}; the
primitive moves it needs are the ones just executed.

\emph{Hardware record.} Run on \texttt{ibm\_marrakesh} (12 June 2026, 13:48 UTC, 8192 shots,
SamplerV2), with the Bell caps as postselected Bell-basis measurements; the heralding rates landed
on their ideals ($0.259$ and $0.267$ against $\tfrac14$ for the GHZ fusion settings, $0.280$
against $5/18$ for W, $0.033$ and $0.030$ against $1/32$ for the triangle). The fused GHZ$_4$
showed population $P(0000)+P(1111) = 0.925$ and parity
$\langle X^{\otimes4}\rangle = +0.837$, a two-setting fidelity estimate near $0.88$ --- for a
state built not by direct preparation but by contracting two GHZ$_3$ blocks through a cap. The W
contraction placed $91\%$ of its postselected weight on the predicted five-state set, each
component within $0.02$ of its ideal $0.2$, and $7.6\%$ on $\mathrm{W}_4$'s single-excitation
strings --- the level fully accounted for by the single bit-flip noise independently visible in
the GHZ run, so the fusion hypothesis $\mathrm{W}\circ\mathrm{W} = \mathrm{W}_4$, which would put
\emph{all} the weight there, is excluded by better than ten to one. The triangle's free legs
returned $P(000)+P(111) = 0.970$ and $\langle X^{\otimes3}\rangle = +0.818$ on roughly $250$
surviving events (statistical precision $\pm0.06$ on the parity), a two-setting fidelity estimate
near $0.89$: the baryon assembled from the wiring on a physical processor. Two features of the
error anatomy are worth recording. The dominant leakage in the fused GHZ$_4$ is the correlated
pair $0011$/$1100$ at $4.5\%$ --- both halves internally intact but mutually anti-aligned, the
signature of a flip on a cap qubit corrupting the herald rather than of damage to the GHZ blocks.
And the triangle's population is \emph{cleaner} than the fusion's despite the deeper circuit,
because postselection acts as partial error detection: shots corrupted on cap qubits are rejected
rather than counted --- the cap discarding corrupted contractions is a pleasing physical echo of
what the cap means in the calculus. The stated upgrade is the Pauli-frame correction: keeping all
Bell outcomes per cap instead of postselecting on $00$ recovers a factor of $32$ in triangle
statistics at no additional quantum time.

\subsection{The meson skeleton}
\label{app:qiskit-meson}

The baryon was a closed triangle of colour edges; the meson is the two-node loop: a quark and an
antiquark as GHZ nodes joined by \emph{two} Bell caps, one free leg each. (At skeleton level the
antiquark's conjugation is trivial, the GHZ amplitudes being real; the charge and flavour structure
of a physical meson lives on the $A$ and $B$ qubits, deliberately excluded here.) The executed
contraction:

\begin{lstlisting}[language=Python,basicstyle=\small\ttfamily]
GHZ--GHZ two-edge loop: output on free legs = (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2)
   fidelity with Bell pair = 1.000000   (weight 0.1250)
W--W   two-edge loop: output = (2|00> + |11>)/sqrt(5)
   fidelity with Bell pair = 0.900000   (weight 0.1389)
\end{lstlisting}

The meson's wiring closes onto \emph{exactly} the Bell pair --- the minimal singlet, the canonical
maximally entangled orbit of \S\ref{sec:canonical-orbits} --- just as the baryon's wiring closed
onto $\mathrm{GHZ}_3$. The same double contraction on W nodes produces a tilted,
non-maximally-entangled pair, off the canonical orbit: once again the binding sector composes onto
canonical objects and the non-binding sector does not. What this computation is, and is not: it is
the meson's \emph{internal-structure skeleton}, the network on which the framework's proposed
composite-mass programme (the geodesic length of the closed network, with the glueball's
$\mathrm{C}$-only loop as the simpler first target) would operate; it is not a meson mass, which is
dominated by the colour-field dynamics of the deferred confinement sector and additionally awaits
the invariant-to-mass map of Open Problem~\ref{op:associator-geodesic}. The skeleton being in hand
means the network side of that future computation is already built.

\emph{Hardware record.} Run on \texttt{ibm\_kingston} (13 June 2026, 06:08 UTC, 8192 shots,
SamplerV2), with the two Bell caps as postselected Bell-basis measurements. The GHZ skeleton's free
legs returned population $P(00)+P(11) = 0.949$ and parity $\langle XX\rangle = +0.835$, a
two-setting fidelity estimate near $0.89$ with the Bell pair --- for a state built by contracting
two GHZ$_3$ nodes through two caps in series. The W skeleton, on the same device, returned a
diagonal population split of $0.80/0.20$ (renormalised onto the diagonal subspace), landing on the
predicted tilt, against the GHZ skeleton's near-balanced split: the two node types produce visibly
different free-leg populations in the direction the dictionary requires, the binding sector onto the
canonical pair and the non-binding sector onto a tilted one. Two features of the error anatomy are
diagnostic. The GHZ diagonal split was itself imbalanced ($0.546/0.403$ before renormalisation,
where the ideal is symmetric) --- the signature of amplitude damping, $\lvert1\rangle$ relaxing
toward $\lvert0\rangle$ during the circuit, which points the same way in both circuits and deepens
the W tilt as expected. And, as in Appendix~\ref{app:qiskit-composition}, the populations are clean
while the parities carry the loss, because postselection on the cap bits rejects shots corrupted on
the cap qubits but not the slow relaxation on the surviving free legs.

\emph{Hardware design.} Six qubits, two Bell-basis cap measurements (postselection weight $1/8$,
roughly a thousand surviving events at $8192$ shots), and the standard pair of witnesses on the two
free legs: the $Z$-distribution (ideal: $00$ and $11$ at $\tfrac12$ each for GHZ nodes; $0.8/0.2$
for the W contrast, whose tilt is itself the observable) and the $\langle XX\rangle$ parity
(ideal $+1$ for the Bell pair, $+0.8$ for the tilted pair). The companion notebook carries the
hardware path.
